## The Fighting Temeraire: a sunset says goodbye
sources:
    - id: ng
      title: The Fighting Temeraire (NG524)
      authors: The National Gallery, London
      year: 2024
      finding: Shown at the Royal Academy in 1839; the 98-gun Temeraire of Trafalgar, sold in 1838 and towed up the Thames to Rotherhithe to be broken up; Turner restored her masts, painted her hull white and gold, showed one tug, set the sun in the wrong direction for a ship going up the Thames; the crescent moon; clouds echo the tug's smoke; 'his Darling'
      url: https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/joseph-mallord-william-turner-the-fighting-temeraire
    - id: wiki
      title: The Fighting Temeraire
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: Towed by two tugs, one shown; masts removed before the sale; uncertain whether Turner saw the tow; refused to sell, left to the nation (Turner Bequest, 1856)
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Fighting_Temeraire

    card title "The Fighting Temeraire"

    image art cites ["ng","wiki"] credit "J. M. W. Turner, 1839. National Gallery, London. Public domain." x 500 y 330 width 880 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/The_Fighting_Temeraire%2C_JMW_Turner%2C_National_Gallery.jpg/1920px-The_Fighting_Temeraire%2C_JMW_Turner%2C_National_Gallery.jpg look screen regions.buoy [0.815,0.8,0.055,0.1] regions.convoy [0.14,0.2,0.29,0.6] regions.cool_side [0,0,0.5,1] regions.far_ship [0.44,0.66,0.04,0.075] regions.flag [0.305,0.37,0.025,0.04] regions.funnel [0.355,0.55,0.02,0.17] regions.hull [0.145,0.6,0.165,0.17] regions.masts [0.14,0.2,0.16,0.42] regions.moon [0.085,0.16,0.025,0.04] regions.red_clouds [0.6,0.56,0.3,0.12] regions.reflection [0.735,0.73,0.08,0.25] regions.ship [0.14,0.2,0.18,0.58] regions.smoke [0.245,0.42,0.13,0.17] regions.sun [0.755,0.675,0.05,0.06] regions.sun_column [0.6,0.5,0.36,0.48] regions.sun_glow [0.66,0.56,0.24,0.22] regions.sunset [0.56,0.15,0.44,0.62] regions.tug [0.26,0.57,0.165,0.22] regions.warm_side [0.5,0,0.5,1]

B: A pale old warship, a little black boat, and a sky on fire. Can a sunset really say goodbye?
    highlight art:ship art:tug art:sunset strength soft

A: Turner thought so. This is his Fighting Temeraire, shown at the Royal Academy in London in 1839.
    portrait turner x 175 y 555 width 90 of "J. M. W. Turner" cameo true

    erase turner

B: So who's the grand old lady on the left?
    point at.ref art:ship gesture glow

A: The Temeraire: a warship of ninety-eight guns, and one of the ships that fought at Trafalgar in 1805.
    highlight art:ship

A: But in 1838 the navy sold her, and steam tugs towed her up the Thames to be broken up.
    highlight art:ship art:tug

    camera on ["art@[0.12,0.36,0.36,0.46]"]

B: That little black thing is doing the towing? It's a beetle pulling a cathedral.
    highlight art:tug lift true

A: A beetle with a steam engine, and the future on its side: most see it as steam, hauling the age of sail to the scrapyard.
    highlight art:funnel art:smoke

    camera on ["art@[0.08,0.16,0.32,0.5]"]

A: Turner bent the facts to make the point. The real s (@highlight art:masts@)hip had lost her masts; he gave them back.

    camera reset true

A: And he painted her hull pale and gold, so she floats like a ghost of herself.
    highlight art:hull

B: Then she's a ghost before she's even gone.
    focus art:ship strength strong rest grey

    ask "Tap where the light comes from." spot sun on art voice a
        intro: Now the question we ask every painting: where is the light coming from?
        right: Yes, right there.
        wrong: Look low on the right, just above the water.

A: From the sun, low on the right, sinking into the haze, and the whole sky catches fire around it.
    highlight art:sunset lift true

B: And the left side stays cool: blue sky, grey mist, and the ship.
    highlight art:cool_side strength soft lift true

    camera on ["art@[0.03,0.1,0.16,0.14]"]

B: Hang on, is that a moon up there?
    highlight art:moon effect ring

    camera reset true

A: A thin crescent, rising over her. Sun going down on one side, moon coming up on the other: a changing of the guard.
    blocking false

    pause 1.6

    highlight art:sun effect ring lift true duration 2.4

    highlight art:moon effect ring lift true duration 2.6

A: Even the red clouds by the sun pick up the rusty colour of the tug's smoke.
    highlight art:red_clouds art:smoke

B: Surely he was there to watch her go, though?
    highlight art:sunset strength soft lift true

A: Nobody knows if he even saw it. And for a ship goi (@highlight art:convoy strength soft lift true@)ng up the Thames, this sun sits in the wrong part of the sky.

B: So he moved the sun!
    highlight art:sun_glow strength soft lift true

    camera on ["art@[0.5,0.3,0.5,0.6]"]

A: Painters can do that. He set it over her last journey: the day's light goes out as her working life does.
    highlight art:sun_column rest paper lift true

    camera reset true

B: So the sunset isn't the weather. It's the goodbye.
    highlight art:ship strength soft lift true

A: Caravaggio's beam picked out one man.  (@highlight art:sunset lift true@)Turner's sunset sees off a whole age.

    quiz "A film about a coal mine closing ends on the last shift walking home. Which light follows Turner?" voice a
        right: The setting sun: the day ends just as the mine's working life does, so the light says goodbye for it.
        wrong: Turner chose the hour when the day itself is ending.
        * The last shift walks home under noon glare
        * The last shift walks home under stadium floodlights
        * The last shift walks home in grey drizzle
        + The last shift walks home into a setting sun

A: Turner never sold it. He reportedly called it his darling, and left it to the nation.
    highlight art:ship strength soft lift true

